{"id":"a-flanger-named-inside-a-larger-type-is-the-one-that-stands-alone","path":"inferences/roland-sc8850-01/a-flanger-named-inside-a-larger-type-is-the-one-that-stands-alone.json","unitId":"roland-sc8850-01","state":"parked","level":"parked","why":"the claim took its rounds and did not close","verdict":null,"rounds":5,"madeAt":"2026-09-19T16:27:56+00:00","madeBy":"backup/does-a-combinations-flanger-loop-around-its-own-delay.py, backup/read-the-combinations-flanger-comb.py, backup/write-the-combinations-flanger-inference.py, backup/what-rate-a-series-combinations-flanger-runs-at.py, backup/which-held-byte-doubles-the-parallel-flangers-rate.py, backup/which-held-byte-the-doubling-needs.py, backup/what-the-flangers-loop-does-to-its-own-rate.py, backup/add-round-two-to-the-combinations-flanger-inference.py, backup/correct-the-counts-the-modulator-round-reported.py, backup/the-flangers-loop-in-the-combinations-that-carry-no-reading.py, backup/read-the-loop-in-the-combinations-that-carry-no-reading.py, backup/add-round-four-and-park-the-combinations-flanger-inference.py, backup/publish-the-balance-teeth.py, backup/publish-the-pair-readings.py, backup/add-the-two-branches-round-to-the-combinations-flanger-inference.py","about":{"scope":"unit","class":null,"types":["01 23","02 01","02 04","02 07","02 0A","02 0B","11 02"],"addresses":["40 03 03","40 03 06","40 03 08","40 03 0B"],"checkedOnUnits":[]},"title":{"names":[{"name":"Stereo Flanger","page":217,"document":"roland-sc-8850-owners-manual-en"},{"name":"OD→ Flanger","page":219,"document":"roland-sc-8850-owners-manual-en"},{"name":"DS → Flanger","page":219,"document":"roland-sc-8850-owners-manual-en"}],"more":4,"parameters":[],"parametersMore":0,"document":"roland-sc-8850-owners-manual-en"},"printedTypes":[{"type":"01 23","name":"Stereo Flanger"},{"type":"02 01","name":"OD→ Flanger"},{"type":"02 04","name":"DS → Flanger"},{"type":"02 07","name":"EH → Flanger"},{"type":"02 0A","name":"FL → Delay"},{"type":"02 0B","name":"Cho→ Flanger"},{"type":"11 02","name":"Cho / Flanger"}],"supersededBy":null,"retractedBecause":null,"claim":"The delay a type prints `FL Dly` against and the loop it prints `FL Fb` against are, in all six types that print them inside a larger chain, the delay and the loop of the stereo flanger that stands alone. Read as a comb at two settings of the delay byte and on both signs of the loop, 23 of the 24 readings return the single type's figures exactly and the remaining one is refused by its own record's admission rule rather than disagreeing: 0.618 ms at a delay byte of 6 and 1.318 ms at 13 with the loop in phase, 0.624 and 1.296 with it inverted. That holds where the chorus runs into the flanger, where it sits beside it, where an overdrive or a distortion runs into it with no way out of the path, where an enhancer does, and where the flanger itself runs into a delay -- so no arrangement carries a delay line of its own, and on the last of them the pair of bytes is not even at the same address. The teeth are 21.91 dB deep at the shallowest admitted, and each is judged against its own record's bar rather than a shared one -- 10.20 to 22.89 dB across the six, cleared by 1.10 times at the nearest. Each profile scores as a comb of that spacing by at least 1.51 times what the same profile scores read on the other sign -- which is the control a filter sitting somewhere cannot imitate, since a peak scores the same either way. The modulator behind that delay is the single type's too, on the type the chorus runs into it. Its rate byte returns a table over ten settings whose four published ones are the single flanger's to four figures -- 1.6499 against 1.65, 4.9994 against 4.9993, 6.1007 against 6.0974, 9.9991 against 9.9986 Hz -- and the settings between them fall on the same line. Its depth byte buys the single flanger's excursion at every setting either run reads, to within 1.71 per cent against the 0.42 per cent one setting taken twice repeats to. On the type the chorus sits beside, the modulator is not read here: the reading that returns a rate picks a period out of an autocorrelation, and on that type the frequency series of a partial stands taller at twice the rate than at it at most settings of the flanger's feedback byte and carries nothing at the rate at all at the centre of it, which is where every run that is not asking about the loop parks that byte.","named":null,"whyNotNamed":null,"adds":"The single type's claim says the byte is a loop and says what it is around. It says that on one type. Six other types print that same pair, five of them at the same addresses over the same printed ranges, and nothing structural had been read on any of them -- the only claims naming them were the two that run across every type, about what a parameter powers up holding and about what happens to a byte its page prints no state for. What is added is that the stage a combination names after another type is that type's stage, read on the one quantity a delay line cannot fake: where its comb sits. Four of the six were read after the claim was made and against it: three print the pair at the same addresses behind a stage that cannot be taken out of the path, and the fourth prints it five addresses lower because the flanger is the first half rather than the second.","refutedBy":"A comb in any of the six that sits at a spacing the single type does not return, at either delay setting or on either sign of the loop. One reading off the single type's two figures by more than the width the fit reports would say the stage is named after something it is not.","couldHaveBeenRefutedBy":null,"whatItWouldChange":"Rendering either type needs one delay line and one loop, with the milliseconds the single type's table gives, rather than a second table read off these types. Thirty of this unit's sixty-five types are combinations named after others, and this is the second stage to be asked whether the name reaches the block -- after an equaliser section, and with 