"within":"efx-pair/11-02-0C-across-the-balance-byte.json","keys":["readings[value=0].lag_us","readings[value=16].lag_us","readings[value=127].lag_us","floor.across_takes_us","floor.widest_within_a_take_us","settings_asked","held"],"values":[-21.31,-21.279,-1.872,0.046,11.869,[0,16,32,64,96,112,127],[{"address":"40 42 22","bytes":"01"},{"address":"40 12 31","bytes":"00"},{"address":"40 03 03","bytes":"00"},{"address":"40 03 04","bytes":"00"},{"address":"40 03 05","bytes":"00"},{"address":"40 03 07","bytes":"00"},{"address":"40 03 08","bytes":"30"},{"address":"40 03 09","bytes":"13"},{"address":"40 03 0B","bytes":"40"},{"address":"40 03 12","bytes":"40"},{"address":"40 03 13","bytes":"00"},{"address":"40 03 14","bytes":"40"},{"address":"40 03 15","bytes":"7F"},{"address":"40 03 16","bytes":"7F"},{"address":"40 03 0A","bytes":"40"}]]}],"documentRows":[{"file":"documents/roland-sc-8850-owners-manual-en/effect-list.json","rows":["01 23,parameter=3,Pre Dly,0–1.6ms–100ms","01 23,parameter=6,Feedback,-98%–+80%–+98%","02 0B,parameter=6,FL Dly,0–1.6ms–100ms","02 0B,parameter=9,FL Fb,-98%–+80%–+98%","11 02,parameter=6,FL Dly,0–1.6ms–100ms","11 02,parameter=9,FL Fb,-98%–+80%–+98%"]}],"eraPriors":[{"claim":"A combination effect of this period is built from the same blocks the single effects are, because the blocks are code and memory that already exist and a second copy of a delay line costs both.","why":"Carried, not closing. The same prior would have been offered for the bass multi-effect's amp table, and that one reaches a table of its own -- so this is a reason to ask rather than an answer.","wouldBeWrongIf":"Either combination's comb had sat at a spacing of its own. It is wrong somewhere on this unit already: the bass multi-effect's amp entries sit a median 7.7 dB from the same entries read anywhere else, which is wider than that table's own entries are from each other. So the prior is a reason to ask and the comb is what answers."}],"community":[]},"alternatives":[{"reading":"Each combination carries a delay line of its own, tuned to the same printed range.","candidate":null,"standing":false,"ruledOutBy":"Two separately built delays would have to agree with the single type's and with each other at both settings, and the reading resolves them finely enough for that to be a claim: the fit returns 0.618 ms against 1.318 for a delay byte moving from 6 to 13, which is a ratio of 2.133 where the byte's own is 2.167. All eight readings across the two types land on the single type's two figures and on no other.","equivalent":false,"separatedBy":null},{"reading":"The teeth are a filter of the combination's own rather than a loop, and they happen to sit where the single type's do.","candidate":null,"standing":false,"ruledOutBy":"A comb and a peak are told apart by what happens below the printed nought of the feedback byte. Every profile was scored both as an in-phase comb and as an inverting one, and each of the eight scores at least 1.55 times better on the sign its setting implies -- the settings under the centre on the inverting reading and those over it on the in-phase one. A filter sitting somewhere scores the same both ways.","equivalent":false,"separatedBy":null},{"reading":"The chorus in front of or beside the flanger is still in the path, and what was read is the two stages together.","candidate":null,"standing":false,"ruledOutBy":"Each run wrote the chorus out of the path in the way its own arrangement allows -- the series type's chorus balance to its dry end, the parallel type's chorus level to nothing -- and parked its rate and depth as well. A modulated stage left in the path smears a comb into a shelf, which is what the first published reading of a modulated type came back as; these come back as combs of a single spacing.","equivalent":false,"separatedBy":null},{"reading":"The two combinations share one delay line with each other but not with the single type.","candidate":null,"standing":false,"ruledOutBy":"They return the single type's figures, not a third pair. There is nothing left for this reading to be about.","equivalent":false,"separatedBy":null},{"reading":"The half named inside `11 02` agrees on where the comb sits and differs somewhere -- in the balance or in the output stage. The modulator is answered on both combinations, though not by the three coming out identical: read at the feedback byte all three types power up holding, the excursion the depth byte buys differs between them by a fraction of what a different modulator was measured to cost on this machine, so a second modulator is refused and a trimmed one is not.","candidate":null,"standing":false,"ruledOutBy":null,"equivalent":false,"separatedBy":{"observable":"The pitch excursion each type's depth byte buys, read the way the single types' were, and the balance byte's two ends.","predicts":{"one stage throughout":"the combination's depth byte buys the single flanger's excursion at every setting","the same delay under a different modulator":"the excursions differ by more than either repeats to"},"sensitivity_here":"The single types' depth runs repeat to 2.74 per cent and the flanger and the chorus differ there by a ratio from 1.316 to 1.501, so the reading separates two modulators by two orders of magnitude more than it repeats to. The rate the two sides are read at has to be established from the height of the frequency series at a named frequency rather than from the period a picker returns: the two differ by an octave on this type.","margin":48,"why_not_separable":"The two branches left are not the modulator's, and neither is reachable by a reading this archive publishes. Where a balance byte puts the two paths equal is a property of the mixture and no stage here publishes one, so a difference found between the three types by sweeping that byte cannot be cited however many times it is taken. The lag between the unit's two outputs is in the same position: it is a quantity the takes carry and nothing publishes. What is missing on both is a stage rather than a booking, and the takes both questions need are recorded."}}],"reproduces":null,"models":[],"charts":[],"examples":[],"extra":[{"key":"why this is not a round of the single types claim","text":"The claim it tests has had three rounds, which is where this project parks one so that a single type cannot absorb the work. And what is claimed here is about two other types: a finding about `02 0B` and `11 02` filed under a file named after `01 23` is a finding nobody looking for those two would find."},{"key":"the reading was corrected before this was scored","text":"The bar a tooth has to clear is three times the spread across the run's own repeats of one setting, which is the record's own `floor_db`. It was being taken from the bypass control instead. The two are the same size on the single type -- 3.14 dB against 1.37 -- and they are not on a type whose effect-alone path sits far from its dry one, where the control stands 20.38 dB off while the repeats stand 3.71, and teeth 26 dB deep were being refused by it. A bar that tightens as the effect gets louder is backwards. The correction moved nothing the single type's claim quotes: it returns the same two delays, and two settings it had refused now read and return them as well."},{"key":"why the standing alternative carries no run","text":"The model is unchanged. What this recorded is that one of the two readings this archive has into a modulator's depth does not reach one of the types, and that the other fails its own null there -- which moves a queue entry and revises nothing the claim states. A count of rounds that went up for it would spend the last revision this claim is allowed on bookkeeping, and would say the model had been taken further than it has."}],"documents":[{"id":"roland-sc-8850-owners-manual-en","title":"SC-8850 Owner's Manual","publisher":"Roland Corporation","copyright":"1999","printing":"00-7-A3-31N","language":"en","pagesRead":0,"pages":256}]}
